  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWidespread fog lifted into a stubborn stratocu field SE of the\nOhio River which is finally starting to erode. As high pressure\nbuilds in and the low-levels dry out, fog will become more \nanchored to the river valleys over the next few nights, where it\nwill become locally dense. \n\nThe drier airmass and clear skies should promote good\nradiational cooling conditions tonight, away from the foggy\nriver valleys. Temperatures are forecast to settle into the \nupper 30s to lower 40s for most areas, with mountain valleys \ndropping into the low to mid 30s. The Frost Advisory highlights \nthe area with greatest confidence to see frost and was left as-\nis. Temperatures don't appear as cold tomorrow night but there \nstill could be some patchy frost in the most sheltered mountain \nvalleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLonger range forecast models indicate our next rain maker\narriving either late in the upcoming weekend or early next week\nas an area of low pressure lifts out of the Gulf of Mexico into\nthe Tennessee Valley and southern Appalachians. This has\ntropical origins so could bring plentiful rains, but there's\nalso plenty of model uncertainty so will just have to watch how\nthe models handle it over the coming days.",
